<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The whole manifest an Android Library plug-in needs when it ships resources
  and no code. Shape taken from Unity's own first-party androidlib
  (com.unity.mobile.notifications 2.3.2,
  Runtime/Android/Plugins/mobilenotifications.androidlib/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml),
  which declares its package exactly this way. That package keeps it under
  src/main/ because it ships a build.gradle; a BARE androidlib like this one
  has no build.gradle, and Unity generates the module with the manifest and
  res/ at the ROOT — the first CI run of this fixture measured exactly that.

  `package` rather than a `namespace` in a build.gradle of our own: Unity
  2022.3 exports AGP 7.1.2, where the manifest attribute is still the supported
  way for a library module to name itself. AGP 7.3 deprecates it (a warning)
  and AGP 8.0 removes it, so this androidlib is deliberately confined to the
  2022.3 testbed — Testbed6 exports AGP 8.x and would reject it. Unity's own
  package makes the same split: it keeps package= for the AGP 4.0.1 of 2021.3
  and sets `namespace` from build.gradle when the running AGP has it.

  No build.gradle here on purpose. README.md's recipe is manifest + res and
  nothing else, and this androidlib exists to hold that recipe to a real APK;
  adding a build.gradle would prove a different, easier claim.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="com.quickactions.testbed.icons" />
